      // [src_keino_athleticsafrica, src_keino_bristol, src_keino_olympedia | 1972 | federation release + university citation + archive] At the 1972 Games he entered the 3,000 m steeplechase, an event the IAAF release says he had little experience over the barriers in and Bristol says he had rarely run, and won the gold after a sprint finish with compatriot Ben Jipcho, while also winning the 1,500 m silver days later. Taking an untested event at a major Games is documented; the outcome was a win, so the evidence says little about how he handled risks that failed, hence dual_edged and moderate confidence.
      risk_tolerance: [62, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_keino_classic, src_keino_olympedia, src_keino_bristol | 1982-2017 | event-site profile + archive + university citation] The profile bearing his name lists his posts as president of the National Olympic Committee of Kenya from 1999 to 2017, IOC commissions from 1982, chef de mission of Kenya's team at the 1994 Commonwealth Games and 1996 Olympics, and coaching of Kenyan teams in 1976 and 1984; Olympedia and Bristol confirm the Olympic Committee role. Long-held sports-administration posts are documented, but the detailed list comes from a self-affiliated source, the sources say nothing about how he led, and controversies reported around the Kenyan Olympic delegation in 2016 are allegation-level matters excluded from scoring under the living-subject rule. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 66 / 0.55 because the detailed record rests on a self-affiliated event profile and no independent source describes his conduct in office.
      leadership_drive: [62, 0.5, "s", "A"],
